findings.claim:
A with the lock (the drawings are now square, but two faults remain).
(1) Its parts list still sizes the canopy's sheet-metal parts from their turned solids. Each printed size, with the square size after it:
- gripper-brackets 110 x 143 x 44 (square 128 x 82 x 44);
- tray-wall 106 x 37 x 52 (19 x 109 x 52);
- tray-u 118 x 62 x 52 (37 x 117 x 52);
- relief-bracket 41 x 34 x 53 (27 x 38 x 53).
The cause is bom.py, which reads p.solid, not p.detail.
(2) Every lamp-part sheet's datum says "angles from the cables' line (0 deg)", yet the same sheets put A's cables at 105/285 (crown, cap, hub) and the collar's keyed entry at 0 deg. In A with the lock, 0 deg is the lamp's x axis (the keyed slot's entry), not the cables' line.

Weights confirmed. The lane's own weights.py, run on my export, gives:
- today's top 1612 g (center 0.04 off the axis);
- A with the lock 1712 g (0.86 off);
- A without the lock 1693 g (0.03 off);
- B 1590 g (0.04 off);
- the canopy and all in it 1021 g.
In all, 2.61 to 2.73 kg, so NOTES 9's '2.6 to 2.7 kg' (against 11.5.1's 4.54) holds.

findings.evidence:
- The marks are text only:
  - crystal.py:1173 (collar: 'a laser-marked arrow on the top face over the keyed slot's entry (0 deg)');
  - crystal.py:1180 (crown: 'a laser line on its lowest tier's side at 30 deg, over the key pin');
  - NOTES crystal.py:1605;
  - printed in every set (A pdf text ×2, A-without-lock ×2).
- No mesh carries them. crown.stl is unchanged in V10. The collar.stl change is only the detent dimple, 2.6 to 3.1 wide (crystal.py diff), and A with the lock's collar.stl did not change at all.
- Trotec: laser-marking anodized aluminum removes the layer 'to reveal white markings'; black marks need a MOPA laser. https://www.troteclaser.com/en-us/helpcenter/materials/material-usage-hints/black-marking-anodized-aluminum
- Where the line would show:
  - top sheet's level views (camera at azimuth -64.3): 94 deg off, edge-on, so not seen;
  - lock frames 2 and 3: 15 and 30 deg off the camera, so in view;
  - lock frame 5: 120 deg off, behind;
  - so a white mark, once modeled, would land unevenly between the lock and no-lock frames.
- Close-ups such as the site's hemisphere-ember-cap would show it plainly, depending on which way the lamp is turned.
- Fix:
  - set its finish and size in the callouts, for example a fine line cut before anodizing so it stays black like the rest, or a MOPA black mark;
  - say it to the owner (the draft does, as part of the fitting step);
  - model it in pass v2 if it is meant to be seen.

